{"subjectId":"python","id":"dictionaries","title":"Dictionaries","group":"Data Structures","order":16,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>dict</code> maps hashable keys to values with average O(1) lookup, insert and delete — the workhorse of Python.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Hash table</strong> — each key's <code>hash()</code> picks a slot; collisions are resolved by open addressing. Since Python 3.7, dicts <strong>preserve insertion order</strong>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-pencil\"></i>Create, read, update</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>ages = {\"asha\": 25, \"ravi\": 30}\nages[\"kiran\"] = 28                    # insert / update\nprint(ages[\"asha\"])                   # 25\nprint(ages.get(\"zoe\"), ages.get(\"zoe\", 0))   # None 0\nprint(\"ravi\" in ages, len(ages))      # True 3\ndel ages[\"ravi\"]\nprint(ages.pop(\"kiran\"), ages)        # 28 {'asha': 25}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>d[key]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code> for a missing key. Use <code>d.get(key, default)</code>, <code>key in d</code>, <code>setdefault</code>, or a <code>defaultdict</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Iterating</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>scores = {\"math\": 90, \"cs\": 95}\nfor subject, score in scores.items():\n    print(subject, score, end=\"; \")   # math 90; cs 95;\nprint()\nprint(list(scores), list(scores.values()))   # ['math', 'cs'] [90, 95]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Don't add or remove keys while iterating over a dict (<code>RuntimeError</code>). Iterate over <code>list(d)</code> if you must modify it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tools\"></i>Useful methods</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>d.get(k, default)</code></td><td>Value or default — never raises</td></tr><tr><td><code>d.setdefault(k, v)</code></td><td>Insert <code>v</code> if <code>k</code> is missing, then return <code>d[k]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>d.update(other)</code> / <code>d | other</code></td><td>Merge (the right-hand side wins); <code>|</code> returns a new dict (3.9+)</td></tr><tr><td><code>d.pop(k, default)</code></td><td>Remove and return</td></tr><tr><td><code>d.items()</code> / <code>keys()</code> / <code>values()</code></td><td>Live <em>views</em> that reflect later changes</td></tr><tr><td><code>dict.fromkeys(keys, v)</code></td><td>New dict with every key set to <code>v</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>base = {\"theme\": \"light\", \"font\": 14}\nuser = {\"theme\": \"dark\"}\nprint(base | user)                        # {'theme': 'dark', 'font': 14}\ncounts = {}\nfor ch in \"banana\":\n    counts[ch] = counts.get(ch, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)                             # {'b': 1, 'a': 3, 'n': 2}\nprint(max(counts, key=counts.get))        # a</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-key\"></i>What can be a key?</h3>\n<p>Keys must be <strong>hashable</strong>: their hash never changes during their lifetime. Immutable built-ins (<code>int</code>, <code>str</code>, <code>tuple</code> of hashables, <code>frozenset</code>) are hashable; <code>list</code>, <code>dict</code> and <code>set</code> are not.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>grid = {(0, 0): \"start\", (2, 3): \"goal\"}     # tuples make great coordinate keys\nprint(grid[(2, 3)])                          # goal\ntry:\n    {[1, 2]: \"x\"}                            # lists are unhashable\nexcept TypeError as e:\n    print(type(e).__name__)                  # TypeError</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Dictionary patterns</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Frequency count</h3><p><code>Counter(items)</code> or <code>d[x] = d.get(x, 0) + 1</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-folders\"></i>Grouping</h3><p><code>defaultdict(list)</code> then <code>groups[key].append(item)</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Two-sum lookup</h3><p>Store <code>value → index</code> while scanning; check <code>target - x in seen</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Memoization</h3><p>Cache results: <code>memo[args] = result</code> — or use <code>functools.cache</code>.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def two_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = {}                      # value → index\n    for i, x in enumerate(nums):\n        if target - x in seen:\n            return [seen[target - x], i]\n        seen[x] = i\n    return []\n\nprint(two_sum([2, 7, 11, 15], 9))   # [0, 1]</code></pre></figure></section>"}